  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Ticket Stub",
    "overview": "The interface behaves like the paper ticket a counter hands you: one big number, a few plain words, readable at arm's length and held up for the barber to see. Everything that isn't the number or the next action stays quiet (warm off-white paper, near-black ink, hairline borders) so the four status colours can do the talking. A Customer glances at it in bursts between other things, and an Owner taps it with one hand between haircuts. Both need to read it in a second.\n\nThe system is built from stock shadcn/ui (new-york style) on Tailwind 4, re-themed rather than restyled. Identity lives in the theme tokens: a burgundy primary, neutrals tinted toward that burgundy (hue 15), the Clarity City typeface, and a status palette that means the same thing on the Customer page and the Owner's board. Density is low on the Customer page (one column, 24rem max) and moderate on the dashboard (one column, 42rem max). Neither surface ever lays content out in more than one column.\n\nDark mode follows the device and is a full second token set, not an inversion. Every foreground and background pair meets WCAG AA in both modes.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One huge tabular ticket number per Customer screen, never smaller than 4rem.",
      "Status colour is the loudest thing on screen and means the same thing everywhere.",
      "Burgundy marks the shop's own actions; orange-red marks anything destructive, and the two never blur.",
      "Flat, bordered surfaces; depth comes from borders and tone, not shadows.",
      "Every tap target is at least 2.75rem; the most-used action is 3.5rem and pinned within thumb reach."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Status Is Sacred Rule",
        "body": "A status colour means one Ticket status and nothing else. Never use Turn Amber for a warning, or Served Green for a generic success toast.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Reds Rule",
        "body": "Burgundy means \"the shop's next step\"; Ember means \"this ends something\". A destructive action is never burgundy, and a normal action is never Ember.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Token-Only Rule",
        "body": "Colours come from theme tokens only, never fixed Tailwind palette colours or arbitrary values, so a Shop's look can later be changed by overriding tokens. `lib/theme-tokens.test.ts` enforces it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Number Rule",
        "body": "Every ticket number, count and countdown uses tabular figures, so digits don't shift as they tick.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Giant Rule",
        "body": "A screen has at most one display-sized element, and on a Customer screen it is always the ticket number.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
